{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794384621,"nonce":"UwEiS87aTYHAphwmhbeL0Cb4V1A=","title":"Text input click and scroll-into-view measured the text before the caret without kerning","message":"PR 74 made the drawn caret stand where the next glyph is drawn (`TextFace::caret_x`). Two other places in the text input still took the width of the text before the caret on its own, which loses the kerning against the next character: `cursor_position_from_x_with` (click to caret) and `ensure_cursor_visible` (scroll to keep the caret in view).\n\nMeasured with a probe (old formula against the glyph positions of the whole text's layout): \"AVATAR To Wa\" is up to 1.58 px off at 12 px and 3.69 px off at 28 px on macOS (system font), and 0.84 / 1.96 px on Linux (Noto Sans). A click near the midpoint between two glyphs could land one character over, and the scroll stopped short of the drawn caret by the same amount.\n\nFix: one helper, `TextInputData::caret_offset`, used by the drawn caret, the hit test and the scroll. It keeps the width of the preceding characters while an IME composition is under way, as PR 74 does for the caret, because the composition is drawn in runs.\n\nTests (blinc_layout, text_measurer): `a_click_lands_where_the_caret_is_drawn` and `scrolling_follows_the_drawn_caret` fail on PR 74 as it stands and pass with the helper. Both skip without a system font.","files":null}]}